FIRST memory measurement of this port. Peak RSS, port vs C, aarch64/M4 Pro.

commit:  see git log at this file's add
command: tools/mem_gate.sh 6   and   MEM_SIZES="1024 1536 2048" tools/mem_gate.sh 6
method:  /usr/bin/time -l "maximum resident set size" (the sanctioned evidence
         per docs/ACCEPTANCE-CRITERIA.md:112). RSS is a ceiling on what the
         process touched, not an allocator trace.
config:  gradient, qp 32, preset 6, 8-bit 4:2:0.

  size        MP     port KiB     C KiB   port/C
  64x64       0.004      3632      7088     0.51
  256x256     0.062      5744      9312     0.62
  512x512     0.250     12288     15344     0.80
  1024x1024   1.000     37984     36704     1.03
  1536x1536   2.250     78480     71344     1.10
  2048x2048   4.000    125424    119536     1.05

The port uses HALF C's fixed overhead and slightly more per pixel; the two cross
around 1 MP. At 4 MP the port is 122 MiB against C's 117 MiB.

WHY THE FIT IS QUOTED TWICE. Least squares over the SMALL range (64..1024) gives
  port 3.5 MiB fixed + 33.62 MiB/MP ; C 7.3 MiB + 28.72 MiB/MP
and over the LARGE range (1024..2048)
  port 7.2 MiB fixed + 29.20 MiB/MP ; C 8.9 MiB + 26.97 MiB/MP
The slope moves with the range, so neither fit predicts the other end. That is
the concrete reason this project bans extrapolating memory: a "MiB/MP" quoted
from the small sweep would over-predict 4 MP by ~18 MiB. If a number is needed
at a size not in this table, MEASURE it.

NOT MEASURED: allocation counts, transient peaks between samples, bd10 (u16
buffers roughly double the pixel-proportional term but that is a PREDICTION, not
a measurement), and anything on Linux/glibc — this is macOS RSS.
